{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "varcoeff_face_flux_laplacian_lev",
    "description": "Conservative finite-volume lowering of the flux-form column diffusion operator d/dz( K du/dz - F ) on column_nonuniform_1d, with the diffusivity K and a PRESCRIBED interface flux F both supplied on the NLEV+1 interfaces (shape [lev_nodes]) and NO free names. The consumer writes the compound D( {op:-, args:[ {op:*, args:[K, D(u, wrt: lev)]}, F ]}, wrt: lev ) -- diffusive term on the LEFT, coefficient on the LEFT of its product (matching is structural and non-commutative) -- which is the natural spelling of a column conservation law du/dt = d/dz(K du/dz - F): the same one closed-form flux, diffusive minus prescribed, differentiated once. The rule fires on the whole compound at priority 10 (esm-spec 9.6.3 outermost-first compound precedence) and is match-scoped with where {u: {shape: [lev]}, K: {shape: [lev_nodes]}, F: {shape: [lev_nodes]}} (esm-spec 9.6.1), so it fires only on a bare layer field, a bare interface coefficient and a bare interface flux of THIS grid. Interior [2, NLEV-1]: the imported total-flux difference stencil. Boundary layers: the DIFFUSIVE part K du/dz of the flux through a DOMAIN face is not computed from a ghost value; it is dropped, and the total flux through the surface interface (node 1) and the top interface (node NLEV+1) is the prescribed value alone, -F[1] and -F[NLEV+1], exactly as rules/varcoeff_face_laplacian_lev_flux_bc reads its free names kdudz_bot / kdudz_top, so L u[1] = ( K[2] (u[2]-u[1])/(zc[2]-zc[1]) - F[2] + F[1] ) / dz[1] and L u[NLEV] = ( -F[NLEV+1] - K[NLEV] (u[NLEV]-u[NLEV-1])/(zc[NLEV]-zc[NLEV-1]) + F[NLEV] ) / dz[NLEV]. F is positive in the +lev (upward) direction, so a WRF surface sensible-heat flux hfx (W m^-2) into a potential-temperature equation is F[1] = hfx / (rho cp), a moisture flux qfx is F[1] = qfx / rho, a surface stress on a wind component is F[1] = (u_*^2) u/|U|, a rigid model top is F[NLEV+1] = 0, and a PBL scheme's nonlocal (counter-gradient) and entrainment fluxes are the interior entries of F -- which is why a model with several diffused fields (theta, u, v, qv, ...) needs no per-field free names: each field carries its own F, and the one rule serves them all. This rule therefore supersedes the pair varcoeff_face_laplacian_lev_flux_bc + face_flux_D_lev_supplied_faces for any model that would otherwise have to route every prescribed flux through the free-name pair; both remain for the K-only and F-only forms. `zc` and `dz` are the consumer's free-name geometry observeds (grids/column_nonuniform_1d/grid.esm). The operator telescopes exactly: sum_k dz[k] L u[k] = F[1] - F[NLEV+1], so the column integral of u changes only through the two prescribed domain-face fluxes. Second-order in L2 on a smoothly stretched column (supraconvergent); MMS convergence is pinned by problems/heat_column_varcoeff_faceflux_forced, whose errors reproduce problems/heat_column_varcoeff_forced's to round-off because a linear-in-z prescribed flux is discretely exact. A model imports exactly one variable-coefficient second-derivative rule for lev.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:rule",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "grid:column_nonuniform_1d",
      "op:varcoeff_laplacian",
      "axes:lev",
      "order:2",
      "stencil_width:3",
      "bc:supplied_flux",
      "spec:9.6.8"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "LeVeque, R. J. (2007). Finite Difference Methods for Ordinary and Partial Differential Equations. SIAM. Section 2.13 (nonuniform grids)."
      },
      {
        "citation": "Hong, S.-Y., Noh, Y., and Dudhia, J. (2006). A new vertical diffusion package with an explicit treatment of entrainment processes. Mon. Wea. Rev. 134, 2318-2341. (Surface, counter-gradient and entrainment fluxes of the YSU scheme as interface fluxes.)",
        "doi": "10.1175/MWR3199.1"
      }
    ]
  },
